<%#
    The theme, for the <head> of every page.

    Included once per view, so the palette and the way to change it are defined in
    one place rather than repeated and drifting.

    THREE PARTS, each for a reason:

      1. The stylesheets. palette.css carries the 22 palettes as variables,
         app.css maps them onto semantic roles and holds the components.
      2. The inline script. The server sets data-theme from the cookie, so a
         stored choice is already correct on the first paint. For the
         system-preference case the server cannot know the OS setting, so this
         script reads it and picks the matching palette BEFORE the body is parsed.
         That is what keeps the flash off the screen rather than making it brief.
      3. Nothing here reads localStorage: the cookie is the one source of truth,
         which is why the server could render the right theme in the first place.

      THE ANALYTICS TAG IS `defer`, AND THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL. Part 2 above must run before the body
      is parsed, so a blocking or competing script here would reintroduce the paint flash it
      exists to prevent. `defer` also means an unreachable host cannot hold up the page: the
      browser fetches it in parallel and the page renders whether it answers or not.
 
      THE COOKIE NOTICE IS INCLUDED FROM HERE, at the end, and that is the same reasoning as
      the picker above: it is site chrome rather than one page's content, and it must appear
      wherever the site does. It used to be inline in `admin-login.ejs`, so every other page
      rendered no banner at all - which the owner met as "not clickable", there being nothing
      to click. Nothing about the notice is page-specific: every page sets the same two
      cookies through this same include, so every page owes the reader the same notice.
%>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= basePath %>/css/palette.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= basePath %>/css/app.css">
<%# Page-specific sheets, so a page pays only for what it renders. %>
<% if (typeof pageStyles !== 'undefined') { %>
    <% pageStyles.forEach(function (sheet) { %>
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= basePath %>/css/<%= sheet %>.css">
    <% }); %>
<% } %>
<script>
    // Runs before the body renders, so no frame is painted in the wrong theme.
    // Only needed when the choice is "follow system"; a stored palette is already
    // on <html> from the server.
    (function () {
        var FOLLOW = 'system';
        var root = document.documentElement;

        if (root.getAttribute('data-theme') !== FOLLOW) return;

        // The ids match lib/palettes.js: Catppuccin and Catppuccin Latte, the same
        // palette in the two modes.
        try {
            var dark = window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
            root.setAttribute('data-theme', dark ? '<%= theme.systemDarkId %>' : '<%= theme.systemLightId %>');
        } catch (err) {
            // matchMedia unavailable: palette.css already applied the dark pair for
            // the "system" category, so there is a usable theme and nothing to do.
        }
    })();
</script>

<%#
    ANONYMOUS PAGE-VIEW STATISTICS. It counts a page view with no cookie and nothing that identifies
    a visitor, so there is no consent control: there is no cookie to consent to.

    `analytics: false` TURNS IT OFF, AND THE VIEW THAT CARRIES THE CREDENTIAL DECLARES IT. The counting
    script sends `location.href` as the page URL, and on `/q/<token>` the participant's 90-day bearer
    credential is a PATH SEGMENT of that URL: every page view would hand their interview link to the
    analytics host. `views/audit.ejs` and `views/participant-token.ejs` therefore pass
    `analytics: false` in their own `theme-head` include, so A NEW ROUTE THAT RENDERS EITHER OF THEM
    INHERITS THE OPT-OUT without anybody remembering to set a flag.

    THE ROUTES SET IT TOO, and that is deliberate rather than redundant. MEASURED, the include alone is
    enough for every path that renders those views today; the route calls are a second lock on the same
    door, and they are what the exclusion tests exercise. Two independent statements that a credential
    page is not counted is the correct number for this one.

    COUNTING ITSELF STILL DEFAULTS TO ON, because a page nobody counts is invisible and that is the
    worse failure. The two questions are separate: is this page counted, and does this page carry a
    credential. One flag answers both only because a credential page must never be counted.

    NOT decided by sniffing the URL in the browser, which would break the moment a route is renamed.

    `data-exclude-search` strips the QUERY STRING, for the demo link, whose token is one.

    APPENDED BY SCRIPT RATHER THAN WRITTEN INTO THE HEAD, and it is measured: a `defer` script in the
    head still holds up the parser finishing the head, and `DOMContentLoaded` waits for it. With the
    analytics host accepting the connection and never answering, the page stalled at `interactive`
    with only the cookie banner rendered, after 12 seconds. Appended after parsing, the page is
    complete before the request is made.
%>
<% if (typeof analytics === 'undefined' || analytics) { %>
<script>
    // APPENDED AFTER PARSING, so a slow or silent statistics host cannot delay the page.
    (function () {
        function addAnalytics() {
            var s = document.createElement('script');
            s.defer = true;
            s.src = 'https://stats.thebeard.be/script.js';
            s.setAttribute('data-website-id', '89c983d9-1018-40ee-a3bd-48c54b691f7f');
            s.setAttribute('data-exclude-search', 'true');
            (document.body || document.documentElement).appendChild(s);
        }
        if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
            document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', addAnalytics);
        } else {
            addAnalytics();
        }
    })();
</script>
<% } %>

<%- include('cookie-notice') %>
